{"38938":{"h":10,"osm":"way/208273057","kind":"pub","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/The_Cadland_-_geograph.org.uk_-_6445352.jpg","credit":{"file":"The Cadland - geograph.org.uk - 6445352.jpg","year":2020,"author":"Andrew Abbott","licence":"CC BY-SA 2.0"},"stories":[{"layer":"chilwell","text":"The Cadland inn is named after the racehorse that won the Derby in 1828.","source":{"title":"Chilwell","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chilwell","kind":"Wikipedia"},"when":"1828","whenSrc":{"title":"Chilwell","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chilwell","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"The Cadland"}],"sum":"The Cadland is a pub on High Road.","sumSrc":{"title":"OpenStreetMap","kind":"Written from the map's data"},"open":[{"what":"pub","web":"https://www.emberinns.co.uk/nationalsearch/eastandwestmidlands/the-cadland-chilwell"}],"council":"Broxtowe Borough Council","xy":[-1.23504,52.91496]},"65169":{"today":"The Ferry Inn","y":1680,"f":"century","src":"Historic England (via Wikipedia)","dt":"Late 17th century","h":10,"osm":"way/29385991","kind":"pub","listEntry":1270746,"listUrl":"https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1270746","qid":"Q26560764","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/The%20Ferry%20Inn%2C%20Wilford%20-%20geograph.org.uk%20-%201217922.jpg","cat":"Ferry Inn, Wilford","stories":[{"layer":"wilford","text":"The inn was made from two houses and an outbuilding of the late 17th century, and is listed Grade II. Wilford stayed a village until the later 19th century.[^s1][^s2] With woods around it and riverside amenities such as the Ferry Inn, it attracted many visitors from Nottingham.[^s1] The Notts County goalkeeper Albert Iremonger was its landlord until his death in 1958.[^s1]","source":{"title":"Wilford","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wilford","kind":"Wikipedia"},"sources":[{"title":"Listed buildings in Nottingham (Clifton North ward)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Listed_buildings_in_Nottingham_(Clifton_North_ward)","kind":"Wikipedia"}],"cites":{"s1":{"title":"Wilford","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wilford","kind":"Wikipedia"},"s2":{"title":"Listed buildings in Nottingham (Clifton North ward)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Listed_buildings_in_Nottingham_(Clifton_North_ward)","kind":"Wikipedia"}},"weblinks":[["Albert Iremonger","note:Albert Iremonger"]],"links":["Albert Iremonger"],"when":"17th century","whenSrc":{"title":"Listed buildings in Nottingham (Clifton North ward)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Listed_buildings_in_Nottingham_(Clifton_North_ward)","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"Ferry Inn","see":[["Wilford Toll Bridge","69731"]]},{"layer":"people","text":"Born in Wilford, the 6 ft 5 in goalkeeper played 564 matches for Notts County.[^s1][^s2] In the 1940s he ran the Ferry Inn.[^s1][^s2]","source":{"title":"Albert Iremonger","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albert_Iremonger","kind":"Wikipedia"},"who":"Albert Iremonger","sources":[{"title":"Wilford","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wilford","kind":"Wikipedia"}],"cites":{"s1":{"title":"Albert Iremonger","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albert_Iremonger","kind":"Wikipedia"},"s2":{"title":"Wilford","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wilford","kind":"Wikipedia"}},"own":1}],"sum":"Ferry Inn, on Main Road. Today it houses The Ferry Inn. The building dates from the 17th century, according to Historic England (via Wikipedia). It is Grade II listed.","sumSrc":{"title":"OpenStreetMap · Historic England","kind":"Written from the map's data"},"open":[{"what":"pub","web":"https://www.chefandbrewer.com/pubs/nottinghamshire/ferry-inn"}],"council":"Nottingham City Council","coll":[{"title":"Wilford","url":"https://picturenottingham.co.uk/image-themes/wilford.html"}],"xy":[-1.15717,52.93482]},"73370":{"where":"14 Park Terrace","h":8,"osm":"way/243696651","kind":"residential","stories":[{"layer":"people","text":"Tinsley Lindley, who played for Nottingham Forest and England and became a county court judge, lived here with his family at 14 Park Terrace.","source":{"title":"Tinsley Lindley","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tinsley_Lindley","kind":"Wikipedia"},"who":"Tinsley Lindley","when":"1865","whenSrc":{"title":"Tinsley Lindley","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tinsley_Lindley","kind":"Wikipedia"},"own":1}],"council":"Nottingham City Council","xy":[-1.15859,52.95222]},"74549":{"h":10,"osm":"way/16905408","qid":"Q1128250","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/National%20Ice%20Centre%20-%20Trent%20FM%20Arena.jpg","article":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Ice_Centre","cat":"National Ice Centre","stories":[{"layer":"people","text":"The ice dancers Jayne Torvill and Christopher Dean come from Nottingham.[^s1][^s2][^s3] The square outside the ice centre is named Bolero Square after their Olympic routine.[^s1][^s2][^s3] Jayne Torvill was born in Clifton on 7 October 1957 and worked as an insurance clerk at Norwich Union before skating full-time; at Sarajevo in 1984 she and Christopher Dean won the gold with their free dance to Ravel's Boléro, which earned twelve perfect 6.0 marks.[^s3]","source":{"title":"Torvill and Dean","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Torvill_and_Dean","kind":"Wikipedia"},"who":"Torvill and Dean","sources":[{"title":"National Ice Centre","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Ice_Centre","kind":"Wikipedia"},{"title":"Jayne Torvill","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jayne_Torvill","kind":"Wikipedia"}],"cites":{"s1":{"title":"Torvill and Dean","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Torvill_and_Dean","kind":"Wikipedia"},"s2":{"title":"National Ice Centre","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Ice_Centre","kind":"Wikipedia"},"s3":{"title":"Jayne Torvill","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jayne_Torvill","kind":"Wikipedia"}},"own":1}],"sum":"The National Ice Centre is located in Nottingham, England. It is situated just east of the city centre, close to the historic Lace Market area. The NIC was the first twin Olympic-sized ice pad facility in the UK, \"heralding a new era in the development of ice skating\".","sumSrc":{"title":"National Ice Centre","kind":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Ice_Centre"},"open":[{"name":"Motorpoint Arena","what":"theatre","web":"https://www.motorpointarenanottingham.com"}],"council":"Nottingham City Council","xy":[-1.13951,52.95265]},"235918":{"where":"191 Mansfield Road","h":10.6,"levels":"3","osm":"way/116531316","kind":"retail","stories":[{"layer":"people","text":"Herbert Kilpin, a butcher's son, grew up here with nine older siblings, at what was then 129 Mansfield Road. He became the main founding father of AC Milan.","source":{"title":"Herbert Kilpin","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Herbert_Kilpin","kind":"Wikipedia"},"who":"Herbert Kilpin","when":"1870","whenSrc":{"title":"Herbert Kilpin","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Herbert_Kilpin","kind":"Wikipedia"},"own":1},{"layer":"people","pin":"p85","who":"Herbert Kilpin"}],"council":"Nottingham City Council","xy":[-1.15171,52.96287]},"303750":{"h":10,"osm":"way/77810521","kind":"grandstand","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/The_Brian_Clough_Stand_at_the_City_Ground_-_geograph.org.uk_-_8354462.jpg","credit":{"file":"The Brian Clough Stand at the City Ground - geograph.org.uk - 8354462.jpg","year":2026,"author":"Stephen Craven","licence":"CC BY-SA 2.0"},"stories":[{"layer":"people","text":"Brian Clough managed Nottingham Forest at the City Ground.[^s1][^s2]","source":{"title":"Brian Clough","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brian_Clough","kind":"Wikipedia"},"who":"Brian Clough","sources":[{"title":"City Ground","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/City_Ground","kind":"Wikipedia"}],"cites":{"s1":{"title":"Brian Clough","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brian_Clough","kind":"Wikipedia"},"s2":{"title":"City Ground","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/City_Ground","kind":"Wikipedia"}},"when":"1935","whenSrc":{"title":"Brian Clough","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brian_Clough","kind":"Wikipedia"},"own":1}],"council":"Rushcliffe Borough Council","xy":[-1.13297,52.94057]},"303826":{"h":10.6,"levels":"3","osm":"way/79093218","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Benchmark_on_the_Trent_Bridge_Inn_-_geograph.org.uk_-_3212702.jpg","credit":{"file":"Benchmark on the Trent Bridge Inn - geograph.org.uk - 3212702.jpg","year":2012,"author":"Roger Templeman","licence":"CC BY-SA 2.0"},"stories":[{"layer":"bridgford","text":"The inn stood by the bridge before any cricket was played here. The cricketer William Clarke married its landlady, Mary Chapman, and in 1838 they had the field behind it cleared and fenced for the first match on what became Trent Bridge. Clarke left for London in 1845, and his stepson John Chapman ran the pub and the ground. After the First World War the Musters family, who owned both, sold them to the cricket club.","source":{"title":"Trent Bridge Inn","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trent_Bridge_Inn","kind":"Wikipedia"},"weblinks":[["William Clarke","note:William Clarke"]],"links":["William Clarke"],"when":"1838","whenSrc":{"title":"Trent Bridge Inn","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trent_Bridge_Inn","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"The Trent Bridge Inn","see":[["Trent Bridge cricket ground","p735"]]}],"sum":"The Trent Bridge Inn is a pub on Radcliffe Road.","sumSrc":{"title":"OpenStreetMap","kind":"Written from the map's data"},"open":[{"what":"pub","oh":"Mo,Tu,We,Th,Su 08:00-24:00; Fr,Sa 08:00-25:00","web":"https://www.jdwetherspoon.com/pubs/all-pubs/england/nottinghamshire/the-trent-bridge-inn-nottingham"}],"council":"Rushcliffe Borough Council","coll":[{"title":"Trent Bridge","url":"https://picturenottingham.co.uk/image-themes/trent-bridge.html"}],"xy":[-1.13414,52.93719]}}